# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/maps/capital_peers.grim
# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after "="; keep {tokens}.
# "# en:" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;
# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.


@event vaneshade_practice_room
# en: The Room Upstairs
title [06f2f6] = 楼上的房间
# en: {The footman takes you not to a drawing room but up two flights, to a long bare room with a scorched floor. Lyra is practising. In the corner are a camp bed, a stack of books and one candle. "My bedroom," she says, following your eyes, "is downstairs. It's very nice. Visitors like it."|Up the two flights again to the scorched room. Lyra is already in her duelling clothes{rel(lyra) < 10: , and looks at you as if you were a new kind of target|}. "You came back," she says. "Nobody comes back up here."|The practice room again. Lyra has pushed the camp bed against the wall to make room for two duellists, which she says is for the footwork. "Well?" she says, rolling up her sleeves.}
text [36d36f] = {侍从没把你领进会客厅，而是领着你上了两层楼，来到一间又长又空、地板焦黑的房间。莱拉正在练习。角落里有一张行军床、一摞书和一支蜡烛。“我的卧室，”她顺着你的目光说，“在楼下。很漂亮。客人都喜欢。”|又上了两层楼，来到那间烧焦的房间。莱拉已经换好了决斗服{rel(lyra) < 10: ，看着你的样子像在看一种新的靶子|}。“你又来了，”她说，“从来没人再上来第二次。”|又是练习室。莱拉把行军床推到了墙边，腾出两个决斗者的地方，她说是为了练步法。“怎么样？”她说着挽起袖子。}
# en: A tall bare practice room in a grand house, the floor scorched in rings, a narrow camp bed and a stack of books in one corner, a young woman in duelling clothes.
scene [f48890] = 一座豪宅里高大空旷的练习室，地板上烧出一圈圈焦痕，角落里一张窄窄的行军床和一摞书，一个穿决斗服的年轻女子。
# en: Spar with her
opt.left.label [ca472c] = 跟她过几招
# en: {She doesn't hold back, so neither do you. An hour later you're both sitting on the scorched floor, singed at the cuffs and aching. "Nobody comes up here," she says. "Mother says the smell gets into the curtains."|She opens with the family flourish and then, halfway, drops it for something of her own. You both end up bruised and breathless on the floor. "Don't tell anyone I improvise," she says.|You spar until the candle's out and your arms shake, across the whole cleared floor. At the door she says, "Same time tomorrow," and it isn't a question.}
opt.left.say.1 [31426c] = {她毫不留手，你也一样。一个钟头后，你们俩都坐在焦黑的地板上，袖口烧焦了，浑身酸疼。“从来没人上来，”她说，“母亲说这股味道会钻进窗帘里。”|她先用家传的起手式，打到一半却丢开了，换成了自己的东西。你们俩最后都青一块紫一块、气喘吁吁地倒在地上。“别告诉别人我会即兴发挥，”她说。|你们在腾空的整片地板上一直对练到蜡烛燃尽、胳膊发抖。在门口，她说：“明天同一时间。”这不是问句。}
# en: Ask about the house
opt.right.label [a9cc20] = 问问这座房子
# en: {"Because downstairs I can hear the house," she says at last, when you ask why she sleeps up here. "Everyone asleep at exactly the right time." She straightens the blanket on the camp bed, which is already straight. You go down feeling the house's quiet too.|She tells you which floorboards her mother uses and which doors are always shut. It's a map of a house, and also of a person. You go down the stairs feeling the weight of all those shut doors.|She asks about your home instead: what you eat, who talks at supper, whether anyone ever shouts. She listens as if it were a lecture. You go down the stairs a little sad for her, and fond.}
opt.right.say.1 [d539a2] = {你问她为什么睡在这儿。“因为在楼下我能听见这座房子，”她终于开口，“所有人都在恰好的时间睡着。”她把行军床上的毯子抚平，其实它本来就是平的。你走下楼时，也感到了这座房子的寂静。|她告诉你母亲走哪几块地板、不走哪几块，哪些门永远关着。这是一座房子的地图，也是一个人的地图。你走下楼梯时，感到了所有那些关着的门的分量。|她反过来问起你的家：你们吃什么，晚饭时谁说话，有没有人大声嚷嚷。她听得像在听课。你走下楼梯时，替她有点难过，又很喜欢她。}

@event vaneshade_kitchen_toast
# en: Nineteen Years of Toast
title [b639af] = 十九年的吐司
# en: Lyra takes you down the servants' stair "as a shortcut", which it isn't. In the kitchen a stout grey-haired woman is already holding out a plate of toast. "Mrs Callow," Lyra says, sitting on a stool as if she's done it all her life. Mrs Callow looks you up and down. "So you're the one in the letters."
text [429107] = 莱拉带你走仆人用的楼梯，说是“抄近路”，其实并不是。厨房里，一个矮壮的灰发妇人已经端着一盘吐司等着了。“卡洛太太，”莱拉说着在凳子上坐下，好像她这辈子都是这么坐的。卡洛太太把你从头打量到脚。“原来信里写的就是你。”
# en: A warm basement kitchen in a great house, copper pans on the walls, a stout grey-haired cook at the range holding a toasting fork, a young noblewoman perched on a kitchen stool.
scene [ebec51] = 一座大宅温暖的地下厨房，墙上挂着铜锅，一个矮壮的灰发厨娘站在灶台边拿着烤叉，一位年轻的贵族小姐坐在厨房凳子上。
# en: Ask Mrs Callow for stories
opt.left.label [4ad2ce] = 请卡洛太太讲讲往事
# en: Mrs Callow tells you about the seven-year-old who set fire to the pudding to prove she could, and the night before her first duel. Lyra says "Mrs Callow" in a warning voice four times. The fourth time she's almost laughing, and still mortified.
opt.left.say.1 [04c24b] = 卡洛太太给你讲了七岁的莱拉为了证明自己能行，把布丁点着了；又讲了她第一次决斗的前一晚。莱拉用警告的语气喊了四遍“卡洛太太”。第四遍时她差点笑出来，却还是窘得不行。
# en: Ask about the letters
opt.right.label [a57af5] = 问问那些信
# en: Mrs Callow fetches a bundle from the dresser drawer: Lyra's letters, one a week since she went away to school at eleven, in a very tidy hand. "About the food, mostly," she says. "And lately, you." Lyra takes the bundle off her, scarlet, puts it back in the drawer, and doesn't deny a word.
opt.right.say.1 [bbd4ab] = 卡洛太太从橱柜抽屉里拿出一沓信：莱拉的信，从她十一岁离家上学起每周一封，字迹工工整整。“大多是说吃的，”她说，“最近嘛，是说你。”莱拉满脸通红地把那沓信从她手里拿走，放回抽屉，一个字也没否认。

@event vaneshade_supper_alone
# en: A Table for Twenty
title [773b58] = 二十人的餐桌
# en: Lady Vaneshade is dining out, as she does every night of the season. Supper at Vaneshade House is served anyway: twenty chairs, eleven courses, one plate. Lyra sits at the end of it like a sentry. "You can stay," she says, "if you like. It's very good. It's always very good."
text [2232a6] = 凡妮希德夫人在外用餐，社交季的每个晚上都是如此。凡妮希德府的晚餐照样上桌：二十把椅子，十一道菜，一只盘子。莱拉坐在长桌尽头，像个哨兵。“你可以留下，”她说，“如果你愿意。很好吃。一直都很好吃。”
# en: A long candlelit dining table set for twenty in a dark formal room, a single young woman in black sitting at the far end before one plate.
scene [810bc7] = 一间昏暗正式的餐厅里，一张点着蜡烛、摆了二十个座位的长餐桌，一个身穿黑衣的年轻女子独自坐在尽头，面前只有一只盘子。
# en: Stay for all eleven courses
opt.left.label [bca3e7] = 留下来吃完十一道菜
# en: Two footmen bring a chair to set beside hers, which has plainly never been done. Eleven courses, mostly in silence, and a long, stiff evening. By the pudding she's explaining the sauce, and her shoulders are down. At the door she says it was adequate, and then winces.
opt.left.say.1 [7fd0df] = 两个侍从搬来一把椅子放在她身边，这显然从来没有过。十一道菜，大多在沉默中吃完，这是个漫长而拘谨的夜晚。到了甜点，她已经在给你讲酱汁了，肩膀也松了下来。在门口，她说这顿饭还算“过得去”，说完自己皱了皱眉。
# en: Take her out for a pie
opt.right.label [4c95ab] = 带她出去吃个馅饼
# en: You buy two hot pies from a stall on the bridges and eat them leaning on the parapet. Lyra has never eaten anything standing in the street. She gets gravy on her glove and stares at it like a new spell. "Don't tell my mother," she says.
opt.right.say.1 [606f53] = 你在桥上的小摊买了两个热馅饼，你们靠着桥栏吃。莱拉从没在街上站着吃过东西。肉汁沾到了她的手套上，她盯着它看，像在看一个新咒语。“别告诉我母亲，”她说。

@event vaneshade_portrait_gallery
# en: The One Who Laughed
title [15c580] = 笑着的那一位
# en: The long gallery at Vaneshade House is two hundred years of black coats and unsmiling mouths. Lyra walks it fast, until the last portrait: an old woman in black, laughing, a little flame curled in her palm like a cat. Lyra stops. "My grandmother," she says. "She was never adequate at anything. She was wonderful."
text [63c39d] = 凡妮希德府的长廊里挂着两百年的黑外套和不笑的嘴。莱拉走得很快，直到最后一幅画像：一位黑衣老妇人在笑，一小团火焰像猫一样蜷在她掌心。莱拉停下了。“我祖母，”她说，“她什么都算不上‘过得去’。她好极了。”
# en: A long gallery of stern dark portraits of nobles in black, and one painting of a laughing old woman with a small flame dancing in her palm, a young woman standing before it.
scene [245a4e] = 一条挂满身着黑衣的严肃贵族肖像的长廊，其中一幅画着一位大笑的老妇人，掌心跳动着一小团火焰，一个年轻女子站在画前。
# en: Ask what she was like
opt.left.label [a15db2] = 问她祖母是什么样的人
# en: "She lit candles by telling them jokes," Lyra says. "Bad ones. Fire likes to be surprised, she said." She tells you three. They're terrible. {knows(lyra.wildfire): "Wildfire was hers," she adds. "You know that. You're the only one who does."|She doesn't say what else her grandmother left her, but you can tell there's something.}
opt.left.say.1 [8df5a3] = “她靠讲笑话点蜡烛，”莱拉说，“很烂的笑话。居然管用。她说火喜欢被吓一跳。”她给你讲了三个。烂透了。{knows(lyra.wildfire): “野火是她的，”她补充道，“你知道的。只有你知道。”|她没说祖母还留给了她什么，但你看得出来还有些别的。}
# en: Light a candle her way
opt.right.label [bd518d] = 照她的法子点蜡烛
# en: You tell the candle on the side table the worst joke you know and push a little magic after it. Nothing. Lyra snorts, which she never does, and the wick catches. "It was laughing at you," she says. "That counts. She always said that counts."
opt.right.say.1 [81af4d] = 你对着边桌上的蜡烛讲了你知道的最烂的笑话，又推了一点魔力过去。什么也没发生。莱拉哼地笑了一声，她从来不这样笑，烛芯着了。“它在笑你，”她说，“也算数。她总说这也算数。”

@event clockworks_long_bench
# en: Properly
title [e5cfc0] = 规规矩矩
# en: {At the Ten Clockworks the whole family works at one long bench, in silence, and nobody has set anything on fire for years. Mir is at the end of it, mending a duchess's carriage clock. Properly. Slowly. Without a single exclamation mark. They look up, see you, and their face lights like a fuse.|Back at the long bench. Mir has moved their stool an inch further from the rest, which in this house is a rebellion. "Pass the three-tooth," they whisper: the first word anyone has spoken in an hour.|The bench again, but today Mir's father has set a second stool beside his own, and Mir is on it, and he is watching their hands. Mir has never looked so frightened, or so pleased.}
text [470c34] = {在滕氏钟表坊，全家人在一条长长的工作台前干活，鸦雀无声，好些年没人点着过任何东西了。米尔坐在长台尽头，修一位公爵夫人的马车钟。规规矩矩。慢慢吞吞。一个感叹号都没有。TA抬起头，看见你，脸一下子亮了起来，像点着了引信。|又回到长工作台前。米尔把凳子往外挪了一寸，在这个家里这就算造反了。“把三齿的递给我，”TA小声说，这是一个钟头里第一句有人说出口的话。|又是工作台，但今天米尔的父亲在自己身边多放了一张凳子，米尔就坐在上面，他看着TA的手。米尔从来没有这么害怕过，也从来没有这么高兴过。}
# en: A long workbench in a clock workshop, a family of artificers in aprons working in silence, a young person with soot-smudged goggles bent very still over a tiny gear.
scene [8907bb] = 钟表作坊里一条长长的工作台，一家穿着围裙的工匠默默干活，一个戴着沾满煤灰护目镜的年轻人一动不动地俯身在一个小齿轮上。
# en: Work beside them, properly
opt.left.label [636ff5] = 在TA身边规规矩矩地干活
# en: {Three hours, one gear, no talking. Mir's father nods at your work once, which Mir says later is "basically a parade". Your back aches and your eyes swim, and you understand Mir a little better. They can work this way. They just can't breathe in it.|Two hours on a mainspring that won't sit, until your neck locks. Mir finishes theirs, then yours, under the bench, so their father won't see. "Properly," they mouth at you, and grin.|You sit very still while Mir's father talks them through an escapement, slowly, the way he talks to clocks. Mir says after that it's the most he has said to them all year. Your back aches from not moving. It's worth every minute.}
opt.left.say.1 [5fd140] = {三个钟头，一个齿轮，一句话都没说。米尔的父亲对你的活儿点了一次头，米尔事后说那“基本等于游行庆祝”。你腰酸眼花，也更懂米尔了一点。TA能这么干活，只是这样喘不过气来。|两个钟头对付一根怎么也装不好的发条，直到你脖子僵住。米尔做完自己的，又在桌子底下替你做完，免得被TA父亲看见。“规规矩矩，”TA冲你做口型，咧嘴一笑。|米尔的父亲慢慢地给TA讲一个擒纵机构，像在对钟说话，你一动不动地坐着；米尔事后说，这是他今年对TA说过最多的话。你一动不动坐得腰都酸了。每一分钟都值。}
# en: Ask to see their real workshop
opt.right.label [50182d] = 请TA带你看真正的工坊
# en: {The real workshop is a cupboard under the attic stairs, with a stool, a lamp, a drawer of borrowed springs and a scorch mark on the ceiling shaped like a bird. "First fire," Mir says proudly. You hit your head twice on the way out. "Everyone does," they say. "It's character-building."|In the cupboard under the attic stairs, Mir shows you this break's project: a clock that runs backwards when it's sad. You crack your head on the lintel. "Character," Mir says sympathetically. "Everyone does it."|Mir has pinned a drawing inside the cupboard door: the long bench, with a small fire at one end of it. "Someday," they say. You bang your elbow getting out, and they laugh, and it's a good laugh.}
opt.right.say.1 [57dad2] = {真正的工坊是阁楼楼梯下的一个柜子，里面有一张凳子、一盏灯、一抽屉“借来的”弹簧，天花板上有一块鸟形的焦痕。“第一场火，”米尔骄傲地说。出来的路上你撞了两次头。“谁都会撞，”TA说，“能磨炼品格。”|在阁楼楼梯下的柜子里，米尔给你看这个假期的作品：一只难过时会倒着走的钟。你的头撞上了门楣。“品格，”米尔同情地说，“谁都会撞。”|米尔在柜门内侧钉了一张画：那条长工作台，一头着着一小团火。“总有一天，”TA说。你出来时撞到了胳膊肘，TA笑了，笑得很好听。}

@event clockworks_drawer
# en: The Drawer Marked M.
title [2cc042] = 标着“米”的抽屉
# en: Mir's father sends you up to his study for a jar of oil. There's a cabinet of narrow drawers, and one, marked only "M.", is open a crack. Inside, oiled and tagged in his neat hand, are a tin bird, a wind-up beetle, a spoon that stirs by itself and dozens more: everything Mir ever made as a child.
text [795267] = 米尔的父亲让你上楼去他书房拿一罐油。那里有一个满是窄抽屉的柜子，其中一个只标着一个“米”字，开着一条缝。里面上了油、系着他工整字迹的标签：一只铁皮鸟、一只上弦甲虫、一把会自己搅动的勺子，还有几十样别的：米尔小时候做过的每一样东西。
# en: A clockmaker's study, a tall cabinet of narrow drawers, one drawer pulled out to show small, carefully oiled childish contraptions with neat paper labels.
scene [638f02] = 一间钟表匠的书房，一个高高的窄抽屉柜，其中一个抽屉拉开着，里面是一件件上过油、系着整齐纸标签的稚气小装置。
# en: Tell Mir what you found
opt.left.label [172ebb] = 告诉米尔你发现了什么
# en: Mir goes up without a word and comes back an hour later with red eyes and the tin bird, wound up and singing. "He labelled the beetle 'first walking mechanism, age six'," they say. "He wrote it down." They're quiet all evening, and so are you, and it's heavy and good.
opt.left.say.1 [4ad923] = 米尔一言不发地上了楼，一个钟头后红着眼睛下来，手里拿着那只铁皮鸟，上了弦，正在唱歌。“他给甲虫写的标签是‘第一个行走机构，六岁’，”TA说，“他写下来了。”TA一晚上都没怎么说话，你也是，沉甸甸的，却很好。
# en: Shut it and say nothing
opt.right.label [3ebf33] = 关上抽屉，什么也不说
# en: You ease the drawer shut and take the oil down. At supper Mir's father asks them, gruffly, what they're working on at the academy, and listens to the whole answer, every tangent. You catch his eye once. He looks away first.
opt.right.say.1 [a9fe7f] = 你轻轻关上抽屉，把油拿下了楼。晚饭时，米尔的父亲粗声问TA在学院做什么，然后把整个回答都听完了，每一个岔题都听了。你和他对视了一眼。他先移开了目光。

@event clockworks_greta
# en: The Proper One
title [8c1002] = 规矩的那一个
# en: Greta Ten is Mir's older sister, the one who'll have the clockworks, and she's very good at it: spotless apron, steady hands, a clock in a hundred pieces that she'll have together by supper. "So you're the friend," she says. "Tell me honestly. Do they really set things on fire up there? On purpose?"
text [38d728] = 格蕾塔·滕是米尔的姐姐，是将来要接手钟表坊的那一个，而且她干得非常好：一尘不染的围裙，稳稳的手，一只拆成上百个零件的钟，晚饭前就能装好。“原来你就是那个朋友，”她说，“老实告诉我。他们在那边真的会点着东西？故意的？”
# en: Two siblings at a clockmaker's bench, an older woman in a spotless apron with tweezers and a younger artificer with singed sleeves, both looking up at a guest.
scene [e48bab] = 钟表匠工作台前的两姐弟，一个系着一尘不染围裙、拿着镊子的年长女子，一个袖子烧焦的年轻工匠，两人都抬头看着一位客人。
# en: Tell her about the fires
opt.left.label [f34276] = 给她讲那些火
# en: You tell her about the owl, the ornithopter and the cauldron that stirs itself. Greta laughs until she has to put down her tweezers, while Mir goes mortified, proud, mortified. On the stairs later she says quietly, "I'd trade the lot for one small fire. Don't tell them."
opt.left.say.1 [69891c] = 你给她讲了猫头鹰、扑翼机和会自己搅拌的坩埚。格蕾塔笑得不得不放下镊子，米尔一会儿窘迫，一会儿骄傲，一会儿又窘迫。后来在楼梯上，她轻声说：“我愿意拿这一切换一场小小的火。别告诉TA。”
# en: Say Mir's the best you know
opt.right.label [44f261] = 说米尔是你见过最好的
# en: "I know," Greta says, a little stiffly, and sets a gear without looking up, and the room goes awkward. Mir is pink to the ears. That evening Greta asks Mir to look at a stuck escapement, and Mir fixes it in a way that makes her say "Oh," and then, "Show me again."
opt.right.say.1 [b2c54c] = “我知道，”格蕾塔说，有点生硬，头也不抬地装上一个齿轮，屋里一下子尴尬起来。米尔的耳朵都红了。那天晚上，格蕾塔请米尔看一个卡住的擒纵机构，米尔用一种让她说“哦”的办法修好了它，然后她说：“再给我看一遍。”

@event ashby_the_chair
# en: The Chair on the Step
title [7dba81] = 台阶上的椅子
# en: {Halfway down Ashby Lane there's an old wooden chair on a doorstep, and Corvin is in it, shuffling. "My grandad's," he says. "Nobody's sat in it since. Nobody but me." Three neighbours call his name from three windows. None of them sounds pleased. All of them sound fond.|Corvin's in the chair again, dealing patience to nobody. "You found the lane twice," he says. "That's twice more than most." A basket comes down from a window on a string; he drops a coin in it without looking up.|The chair, the step, the cards. Tonight there's a stool beside the chair, hauled out from somewhere, and Corvin pats it. "Lane says you can sit," he says. "Don't let it go to your head."}
text [95143e] = {阿什比巷走到一半，一户门前的台阶上摆着一把旧木椅，科尔文就坐在上面洗牌。“我爷爷的，”他说，“那以后没人坐过。除了我。”三个邻居从三扇窗户里喊他的名字。没有一个听起来高兴。每一个听起来都很疼他。|科尔文又坐在椅子上，自己跟自己玩接龙。“你找到这条巷子两回了，”他说，“比大多数人多两回。”一扇窗户里用绳子吊下来一只篮子；他头也不抬地往里丢了一枚硬币。|椅子，台阶，纸牌。今晚椅子旁边多了一张凳子，不知从哪儿拖出来的，科尔文拍了拍它。“巷子说你可以坐，”他说，“别得意。”}
# en: A crooked harbour-town lane at dusk, washing strung between leaning houses, an old wooden chair on a worn doorstep and a young man in it shuffling cards.
scene [adf25e] = 黄昏时港镇一条弯弯曲曲的小巷，倾斜的房子之间晾着衣服，磨旧的门阶上一把旧木椅，一个年轻人坐在上面洗牌。
# en: Ask about the old man
opt.left.label [8150bd] = 问问那位老人
# en: {"He could find a lost thing in any house," Corvin says. "People still knock and ask me where they've put things. Me. As if it ran in the blood." "Does it?" you ask. "Sometimes," he says, and doesn't joke, and the quiet that follows is a sad one, until the lamps come on.|"He said a lost thing wants to be found," Corvin says. "You just stand where it'd look for you." He's quiet a while. You don't push, and the quiet goes sad and comfortable at once.|He tells you the old man's last find: Corvin, aged ten, on a roof, in the rain, though nobody had told him where to look. "Never said how," Corvin says. "Took it with him." You sit with that, sad and close, until the lamps.}
opt.left.say.1 [d180ac] = {“他在哪栋房子里都能找到丢了的东西，”科尔文说，“现在还有人来敲门，问我他们把东西放哪儿了。问我。好像这是遗传的。”“是吗？”你问。“有时候是，”他说，没开玩笑。接下来的沉默有些伤感，一直持续到路灯亮起。|“他说丢了的东西想被找到，”科尔文说，“你只要站在它会来找你的地方。”他沉默了一会儿。你没有追问，那沉默既伤感又自在。|他给你讲老人最后一次找东西：找到十岁的科尔文，在雨里的屋顶上，可没人告诉过他去哪儿找。“从没说过是怎么找到的，”科尔文说，“带走了。”你陪他坐着，伤感又亲近，直到路灯亮起。}
# en: Meet the neighbours
opt.right.label [ac16c7] = 见见邻居们
# en: {The neighbours come down to inspect you, and they're thorough about it. Corvin owes one of them a crown and another a cat; it's complicated. They tell you he was a terrible child. When the watch walks past, all three step in front of him without a word, and the watch walks on.|The neighbours have opinions about you now. One wants to know your prospects; one wants to sell you a hen. It's exhausting. As you go, the hen woman tells Corvin you'll do.|A neighbour brings out a plate of something fried and very hard to chew, and asks after your whole family while you chew it. You pass, wearily. "That's you on the lane, then," Corvin says, as if you'd been sworn in.}
opt.right.say.1 [62c935] = {邻居们下楼来仔细打量你，打量得很彻底。科尔文欠其中一个一个克朗，欠另一个一只猫，说来话长。他们告诉你他小时候是个糟糕的孩子。巡夜人走过时，三个人一声不吭地挡在他前面，巡夜人就走过去了。|邻居们现在对你有看法了。一个想知道你的前途，一个想卖给你一只母鸡。累死人了。你走的时候，卖鸡的女人跟科尔文说你还行。|一个邻居端出一盘炸得很硬、很难嚼的东西，你嚼的时候她把你全家问了个遍。你疲惫地过关了。“那你就算巷子里的人了，”科尔文说，好像你刚宣过誓。}

@event ashby_coins_on_the_sill
# en: Coins on the Sill
title [e0feef] = 窗台上的硬币
# en: Late, Corvin walks you to the end of the lane and stops under a lit window. Inside, a woman is folding someone else's washing. He reaches up, sets a little stack of coins on the sill, quiet as a cat, and steps back into the dark. "My mother," he says. "She doesn't ask where it comes from. I don't go in."
text [631b47] = 夜深了，科尔文送你到巷子尽头，在一扇亮着灯的窗下停住。屋里，一个女人正在叠别人家的衣服。他伸手把一小摞硬币放在窗台上，轻得像只猫，然后退回黑暗里。“我母亲，”他说，“她不问钱是哪儿来的。我不进去。”
# en: A dark harbour lane at night, a young man reaching up to leave a small stack of coins on the sill of a lit window, a student watching from the shadows.
scene [859bcc] = 夜里一条黑暗的港镇小巷，一个年轻人伸手把一小摞硬币放在一扇亮灯窗户的窗台上，一个学生在阴影里看着。
# en: Ask why he doesn't go in
opt.left.label [46a0c8] = 问他为什么不进去
# en: For a long while he says nothing. "Because then she'd ask how I am," he says, "and I'd tell her, and she'd cry, and then I'd make a joke." He makes one now, about windowsills. It isn't funny. You walk back in a heavy silence, the best you've had with him.
opt.left.say.1 [91a850] = 他很久都没说话。“因为那样她就会问我过得怎么样，”他说，“我会告诉她实话，她会哭，然后我就会开个玩笑。”他现在就开了一个，关于窗台的。一点也不好笑。你们在沉重的沉默中走回去，这是你和他之间最好的一次沉默。
# en: Add a coin of your own
opt.right.label [61a724] = 也放一枚你自己的
# en: You set a coin of your own beside his. He looks at it, and at you, and for once has nothing at all to say. Upstairs the woman comes to the window, sees the sill, and stands very still. Corvin pulls you round the corner before she can look down.
opt.right.say.1 [af731a] = 你把一枚自己的硬币放在他的旁边。他看看硬币，又看看你，难得一句话也说不出来。楼上，那个女人走到窗边，看见窗台，一动不动地站着。她还没来得及往下看，科尔文就把你拉到了拐角后面。

@event ashby_lost_ring
# en: Like His Grandad
title [2ec3f1] = 像他爷爷一样
# en: A neighbour comes down the lane at a half-run: her wedding ring is gone, forty years on her finger and gone. "Find it, Corvin," she says. "Like your grandad would." Corvin goes still, then follows her in, and stands in her kitchen without touching anything, just looking. "Help me look," he says to you. "Or don't. Watch."
text [b41b4f] = 一个邻居小跑着从巷子里过来：她的结婚戒指不见了，戴了四十年，不见了。“找找吧，科尔文，”她说，“像你爷爷那样。”科尔文一下子定住了，然后跟她进了屋，站在她的厨房里什么也不碰，只是看。“帮我找，”他对你说，“或者别找。看着。”
# en: A cramped harbour-town kitchen, an anxious older woman wringing her hands, a lean young man looking slowly round the room, a student lifting the lid of a flour jar.
scene [7ad9ee] = 一间狭小的港镇厨房，一个焦急的年长妇人绞着双手，一个精瘦的年轻人慢慢环视屋子，一个学生掀开面粉罐的盖子。
# en: Search the kitchen yourself
opt.left.label [b53dbb] = 你自己在厨房里找
# en: You think about where hands go when they're busy, and lift the lid of the flour jar. The ring is in the flour. The neighbour cries, kisses you on both cheeks and presses a coin on you. Corvin looks at you a long moment. "Huh," he says. "Maybe it's catching."
opt.left.say.1 [6da6a9] = 你想着人忙的时候手会伸到哪里，掀开了面粉罐的盖子。戒指就在面粉里。邻居哭了，在你两边脸上各亲了一下，硬塞给你一枚硬币。科尔文看了你很久。“嗯，”他说，“说不定会传染。”
# en: You turn out every drawer and find string, spoons and a mouse, and feel foolish. Corvin, who hasn't moved, says "Flour jar," and it is. The neighbour kisses him. You get a smaller kiss, for trying, and a broom.
opt.left.say.2 [7c0897] = 你翻遍了每个抽屉，找到绳子、勺子和一只老鼠，觉得自己像个傻瓜。一直没动的科尔文说：“面粉罐。”果然在那儿。邻居亲了他。你得到一个小一点的吻，奖励你努力，外加一把扫帚。
# en: Watch him work
opt.right.label [da186b] = 看他怎么找
# en: He looks a long time, then says, "You were baking yesterday," and goes to the flour jar, and there it is. Outside, he breathes out. "So that's how he did it," he says. "Watching. All those years I thought it was magic." He grins, shaky. "Don't tell. They like it better as magic."
opt.right.say.1 [069f10] = 他看了很久，然后说：“你昨天烤东西了。”走到面粉罐前，戒指就在那儿。出了门，他长出一口气。“原来他是这么做到的，”他说，“看。这么多年我一直以为是魔法。”他咧嘴一笑，有点发抖。“别说出去。他们更喜欢当它是魔法。”

@event oracle_window_seat
# en: A Year From Now
title [0cc79b] = 一年以后
# en: {Seraphine is in the window seat at the top of her mother's stairs, painting the queue. Not as they are, you see over her shoulder; the man in the green coat is greyer, the girl with the letter is laughing. "A year from now," she says, "more or less. They come to hear what they want. I paint what they'll get."|Seraphine's in the window seat again, the sketchbook open at a new face: a woman near the top of the queue clutching a ring box. "She'll get the answer she wants," Seraphine says, "and the one she needs a little after. Same man." She sounds pleased.|The queue is longer this week; there's a duke in it. Seraphine paints him from the back, which is all of him she'll look at. "His future's very loud," she says. "Lots of horses. Sit there, would you? You're quiet."}
text [c9b73e] = {瑟拉芬坐在她母亲楼梯顶上的窗座里，画着排队的人。你从她肩上看过去，画的不是他们现在的样子；穿绿外套的男人头发更灰了，拿着信的女孩在笑。“一年以后，”她说，“差不多吧。他们来听想听的话。我画他们会得到的。”|瑟拉芬又坐在窗座里，速写本翻到一张新面孔：队伍前头一个攥着戒指盒的女人。“她会得到她想要的答案，”瑟拉芬说，“过些时候，再得到她需要的那个。同一个男人。”她听起来很高兴。|这星期队伍更长了，里面有一位公爵。瑟拉芬从背后画他，她只肯看他的背影。“他的未来很吵，”她说，“好多马。你坐那儿好吗？你很安静。”}
# en: A tall window seat at the top of a hushed staircase, a dreamy young woman with a sketchbook painting the anxious people queuing on the stairs below.
scene [697dae] = 一道静悄悄的楼梯顶上高高的窗座，一个神情恍惚的年轻女子拿着速写本，画着楼梯上排队的焦虑人群。
# en: Ask about one of them
opt.left.label [a4b7aa] = 问问其中一个人
# en: {"The man in green has come to ask if his ship comes in," she says. "It doesn't. Mother will tell him it does." She paints a little boat in the corner of his page, sailing off. "He'll be all right. Greyer. He'll open a shop." You watch him, so hopeful, and feel the year coming for him.|"She'll say no to him," Seraphine says of the ring-box woman, "and then, later, yes, and they'll both be glad she waited." She paints a small door in the corner. You watch the woman, so sure of herself, and feel uneasy for her.|"He'll lose a horse race and win a wife," Seraphine says of the duke, "and be cross about the horse." She paints a hoofprint. You laugh, and then don't, because he has turned and is looking straight up at you, and you feel caught out.}
opt.left.say.1 [0ee397] = {“穿绿衣的男人来问他的船能不能回来，”她说，“回不来。母亲会告诉他能。”她在他那页的角落里画了一条小船，正驶向远方。“他会好的。头发灰一些。他会开一家铺子。”你看着他满怀希望的样子，感到那一年正朝他走来。|“她会拒绝他，”瑟拉芬说起那个拿戒指盒的女人，“后来又会答应，他们俩都会庆幸她等了。”她在角落里画了一扇小门。你看着那个那么笃定的女人，替她有些不安。|“他会输一场赛马，赢一位妻子，”瑟拉芬说起那位公爵，“然后为了马生气。”她画了一个马蹄印。你笑了，又笑不出来了，因为他转过身，正直直地抬头看着你，你觉得自己被抓了个正着。}
# en: Help her paint the stairs
opt.right.label [f59a68] = 帮她画楼梯
# en: {She hands you a brush and lets you do the stairs, which will be the same next year. You do them badly. She says they're the best stairs anyone's painted for her, and adds a small figure at the top of them, which is you, a year older, holding a door open.|You do the banisters, carefully, and not too badly. She adds a cat on the landing that isn't there. "There will be," she says.|You paint the window you're both sitting in, and her in it. She looks at your picture a long time. "So that's what I look like from outside," she says, and keeps it.}
opt.right.say.1 [5d1a8b] = {她递给你一支画笔，让你画楼梯，反正明年楼梯也一样。你画得很糟。她说这是有人给她画过的最好的楼梯，又在楼梯顶上添了一个小小的人影，那是你，大了一岁，扶着一扇开着的门。|你画栏杆，画得很小心，也不算太差。她在楼梯平台上添了一只并不在那儿的猫。“会有的，”她说。|你画了你们俩坐着的这扇窗，还有窗里的她。她看着你的画看了很久。“原来从外面看我是这个样子，”她说，然后把画收了起来。}

@event oracle_audience
# en: An Audience
title [6e1e78] = 觐见
# en: The Oracle of Vell sees you because her daughter asked, which, the clerk murmurs, has never happened. She is all silver and stillness, and doesn't look at you but a little to your left, where you'll stand next week. "She listens to you," she says. "Tell her to come home and take her seat. Kings will be grateful. So will I."
text [a5af98] = 维尔神谕肯见你，是因为她女儿开了口，书记员低声说，这从来没有过。她浑身银白，一动不动，不看你，而是看你左边一点，下星期你会站的地方。“她听你的，”她说，“让她回家，接下家族的位子。国王们会感激的。我也会。”
# en: A dim, rich audience room with heavy curtains, a regal woman in silver seated at a table with a single candle, eyes half-closed, a nervous student opposite her.
scene [904137] = 一间昏暗华贵的觐见室，挂着厚重的帘子，一位身着银衣的威严女子坐在只点着一支蜡烛的桌旁，眼睛半闭，对面坐着一个紧张的学生。
# en: Promise to persuade her
opt.left.label [6e6de3] = 答应去劝她
# en: The Oracle smiles exactly as much as she means to, and by evening two uptown houses have heard you're a friend of the family. Seraphine, on the stairs, looks at you once and knows; she always knows. "She asked you too," she says. "Everyone says yes to her. It's all right." It isn't, quite.
opt.left.say.1 [204f35] = 神谕笑得不多不少，正是她想笑的分寸。到了傍晚，上城已经有两户人家听说你是这家的朋友了。瑟拉芬在楼梯上看了你一眼就知道了，她总是知道。“她也问了你，”她说，“谁都会答应她。没关系的。”其实有点关系。
# en: Refuse, to her face
opt.right.label [5abed4] = 当面拒绝她
# en: "No," you say. The Oracle turns her eyes on you for one cold moment, and you feel weighed, and found wanting. "I saw you say that," she says. "I still had to ask." The curtains close. Seraphine is waiting on the stairs, delighted. "Nobody says no to her," she says. "She'll be cross for a week."
opt.right.say.1 [86a8f8] = “不，”你说。神谕把目光转到你身上，冰冷的一瞬间，你觉得自己被称了称，还不够分量。“我看见过你这么说，”她说，“可我还是得问。”帘子合上了。瑟拉芬在楼梯上等着，高兴极了。“从没人对她说不，”她说，“她得生一个星期的气。”

@event oracle_other_painting
# en: The Other Ending
title [967e69] = 另一个结局
# en: When the last petitioner has gone, Seraphine lifts the cushion of the window seat. Underneath is a canvas, face down. She turns it over: herself, older, turning away from a tall silver chair. "Mother has seen me say yes," she says. "{chosen(seraphine_mother, left): Even after I wrote no, she still sees yes.|She always sees me say yes.} So I painted the other one. In case it helps."
text [251b9e] = 最后一个求见者走后，瑟拉芬掀起窗座的坐垫。下面有一幅画，面朝下。她把它翻过来：是她自己，年纪大些，正从一把高高的银椅前转身离开。“母亲看见过我答应，”她说，“{chosen(seraphine_mother, left): 就算我回信说了不，她还是看见我答应。|她总是看见我答应。}所以我画了另一种。万一有用呢。”
# en: A young woman kneeling to lift the cushion of a window seat, a hidden canvas beneath it showing herself turning away from a tall silver chair.
scene [48e6b2] = 一个年轻女子跪着掀起窗座的坐垫，下面藏着一幅画，画中是她自己从一把高高的银椅前转身离开。
# en: "It helps."
opt.left.label [a60c08] = “有用的。”
# en: She looks at the painting as if it were someone she's going to meet. "If I look at it long enough," she says, "it might start to look like a future instead of a wish." She slides it back under the cushion. "Stay a bit longer." You do, until the room goes dark.
opt.left.say.1 [22f6b7] = 她看着那幅画，像在看一个她将要见到的人。“要是我看得够久，”她说，“它也许会开始像一个未来，而不只是一个愿望。”她把画塞回坐垫下面。“再待一会儿。”你待着，一直到屋里暗下来。
# en: "Paint it bigger."
opt.right.label [510204] = “画大一点。”
# en: Next morning you spend more than you should on the biggest canvas in the capital and carry it up the Oracle's stairs past the whole astonished queue. Seraphine laughs, a real laugh, the kind that makes the clerk look up. "It won't fit under the seat," she says. "Good."
opt.right.say.1 [9bd7c6] = 第二天早上，你花了不该花的钱，买下王都最大的一块画布，扛着它从整排目瞪口呆的队伍旁走上神谕的楼梯。瑟拉芬笑了，真正地笑，那种让书记员都抬起头的笑。“座位底下放不下了，”她说，“很好。”

@event hollis_inspection
# en: Boots
title [f4faf2] = 靴子
# en: {In the hall at Hollis House, Bram stands at attention while his father inspects his boots, his collar and his fingernails, exactly as Bram inspects first-years. Bram says "sir" and nothing else; the demerit ledger is in his breast pocket, like a stone. Then Mr Hollis turns to you. "Boots," he says.|Inspection again. Mr Hollis finds a crease in Bram's collar and looks at it for a long, silent moment. Bram stares at the wall. Then the eyes move to you, and to your boots, which you polished, which isn't enough.|The hall, the boots, the silence. This time when his father turns to you, Bram, staring straight ahead, steps half a pace sideways, so that he's standing a little in front of you.}
text [c9c30a] = {在霍利斯宅的门厅里，布拉姆立正站着，他父亲检查他的靴子、衣领和指甲，就像布拉姆检查一年级生那样。布拉姆除了“是，先生”什么也不说；记过簿揣在他胸前口袋里，像块石头。然后霍利斯先生转向你。“靴子，”他说。|又是检查。霍利斯先生在布拉姆的衣领上发现了一道褶，盯着它看了很久，一言不发。布拉姆盯着墙。然后那双眼睛转向你，转向你的靴子，你擦过了，可还不够。|门厅，靴子，沉默。这一次，他父亲转向你时，布拉姆目视前方，往旁边挪了半步，正好挡在你前面一点。}
# en: A narrow, immaculate entrance hall, a stern tall man in an old prefect's sash inspecting a burly young man standing at attention, a guest waiting by the door.
scene [9795af] = 一个窄小而一尘不染的门厅，一个戴着旧级长绶带的严厉高个男人在检查一个立正站着的魁梧年轻人，一位客人在门边等着。
# en: Hold out your boots
opt.left.label [38e5ea] = 伸出你的靴子
# en: {Mr Hollis examines your boots as if they had lied to him. "Passable, for a guest," he says, and goes. Bram lets out a breath you hadn't heard him holding. "Every day he's home," he says. "Since I was six." You feel scrubbed and small. So, you realise, does he.|"Scuffed at the heel," Mr Hollis says, and walks away, and your face burns. Bram, very quietly, hands you a polishing cloth from his pocket. He carries one. Of course he carries one.|Mr Hollis looks at the boots, then at his son half in front of you, for a long time. "Hm," he says, and goes. Bram doesn't move until the study door shuts, and your heart is going like a drum. "Don't," he says. "Don't make it weird."}
opt.left.say.1 [920442] = {霍利斯先生检查你的靴子，好像它们对他撒了谎。“作为客人，还凑合，”他说完就走了。布拉姆吐出一口你都没听见他憋着的气。“他在家的每一天，”他说，“从六岁起。”你觉得自己被刷洗了一遍，又小又窘。你忽然明白，他也一样。|“鞋跟磨了，”霍利斯先生说完走开了，你的脸火辣辣的。布拉姆悄悄从口袋里递给你一块擦鞋布。他随身带着。他当然随身带着。|霍利斯先生看看靴子，又看看挡在你前面一半的儿子，看了很久。“嗯，”他说完就走了。布拉姆一直没动，直到书房门关上，你的心跳得像擂鼓。“别，”他说，“别弄得怪怪的。”}
# en: Ask about his head-boy year
opt.right.label [3ae3d1] = 问问他当级长那一年
# en: {Mr Hollis's face changes, and he talks about his year for an hour, every prize, while you stand there bored to tears and Bram slips away to the kitchen. Later Bram gives you an apple, which from him is a medal.|He talks for an hour about his rival for head boy, whom he has never forgiven, and your attention dies twice. Bram, freed, has polished both your boots by the time you get away.|Today Mr Hollis talks about his own father, and stops halfway, and goes into the study. Bram stares at the shut door. "He always stops there," he says. The long hour has left you tired and strange.}
opt.right.say.1 [8bc81e] = {霍利斯先生的脸色变了，他讲他那一届讲了一个钟头，每一个奖都讲到了，你站在那儿闷得要命，布拉姆溜进了厨房。后来布拉姆给了你一个苹果，从他手里拿到这个就等于勋章。|他讲了一个钟头他竞选级长时的对手，至今没原谅那个人，你走神了两次。重获自由的布拉姆，在你脱身之前已经把你们俩的靴子都擦好了。|今天霍利斯先生讲起了他自己的父亲，讲到一半停住，进了书房。布拉姆盯着关上的门。“他总是讲到那儿就停，”他说。这漫长的一个钟头让你又累又恍惚。}

@event hollis_noon_piano
# en: Scales at Noon
title [a7d973] = 正午的音阶
# en: The maid shows you into the parlour at noon and leaves before you can stop her. Bram is at the piano, playing scales: up, down, perfectly in time, with the face he uses for writing demerits. He sees you and stops dead. "My father played," he says. "I was told to start at six. Nobody said I could stop."
text [8ffee4] = 中午，女仆把你领进客厅就走了，你都来不及拦她。布拉姆在弹钢琴，弹音阶：上行，下行，分毫不差，脸上是他写记过时的表情。他看见你，一下子停住了。“我父亲弹琴，”他说，“我六岁时被叫去开始学。没人说过我可以停。”
# en: A tidy uptown parlour at midday, a burly young man at an upright piano playing scales with grim precision, a startled guest in the doorway.
scene [b9de65] = 中午一间整洁的上城客厅，一个魁梧的年轻人坐在立式钢琴前，一丝不苟地弹着音阶，一位吃惊的客人站在门口。
# en: Ask him to play something real
opt.left.label [61ab1f] = 请他弹点真正的曲子
# en: He glares at you for a whole bar. Then he plays a country dance tune, badly on purpose and then not badly at all. Halfway through he almost smiles. At the end he shuts the lid. "If this is in the Ledger," he says, "I'll know who."
opt.left.say.1 [2efc17] = 他瞪了你一整个小节。然后他弹了一支乡村舞曲，先是故意弹得很糟，后来一点也不糟了。弹到一半他差点笑了。弹完，他合上琴盖。“要是这件事上了《流明纪事报》，”他说，“我知道是谁干的。”
# en: Pretend you heard nothing
opt.right.label [ba4e19] = 假装什么也没听见
# en: "Heard what?" you say, and talk about the weather, awkwardly and at length, until his ears stop being red. He shows you out himself. On the step he says, gruffly, that you can come at noon again if you like. "For the weather," he says.
opt.right.say.1 [c27237] = “听见什么？”你说，然后尴尬地、没完没了地聊天气，直到他的耳朵不再发红。他亲自送你出门。在台阶上，他粗声说你愿意的话中午可以再来。“来聊天气，”他说。

@event hollis_hester
# en: The Hollis Who Got Away
title [55f873] = 逃出去的那个霍利斯
# en: A young woman in a Thornwick scarf is sitting on the stairs of Hollis House eating an apple, while upstairs a door stays loudly shut. "Hester," she says, holding out a sticky hand. "The family disgrace. Thornwick, you know. Father's not speaking to me, so Bram's speaking for two." Bram looks as if he'd like the stairs to swallow him.
text [edce4e] = 一个围着荆木学院围巾的年轻女子坐在霍利斯宅的楼梯上啃苹果，楼上有一扇门响亮地关着。“赫丝特，”她说着伸出一只黏糊糊的手，“家里的耻辱。荆木学院，你懂的。父亲不跟我说话，所以布拉姆得说两个人的份。”布拉姆看起来恨不得楼梯把他吞下去。
# en: A narrow hallway in a very correct house, a sharp-faced young woman in a Thornwick scarf eating an apple on the stairs, a burly younger brother looking caught, a guest by the door.
scene [df00db] = 一座规规矩矩的房子里窄窄的门厅，一个围着荆木学院围巾、面容精明的年轻女子坐在楼梯上啃苹果，一个魁梧的弟弟一脸被抓包的样子，一位客人站在门边。
# en: Sit with Hester
opt.left.label [37d668] = 陪赫丝特坐坐
# en: Hester tells you Bram has written to her every month since she left, and keeps her letters in "that ridiculous ledger". Bram says "Hester" in a voice that could stop a duel, and glares at you for listening. She ruffles his hair. He lets her. Nobody ruffles Bram's hair and lives.
opt.left.say.1 [3a61e3] = 赫丝特告诉你，自从她离家，布拉姆每个月都给她写信，还把她的回信夹在“那本可笑的簿子”里。布拉姆用一种能叫停一场决斗的声音喊了声“赫丝特”，又狠狠瞪了你一眼，怪你在听。她揉乱他的头发。他由着她。没人揉乱布拉姆的头发还能活着。
# en: Get Bram out of the house
opt.right.label [b9856b] = 把布拉姆带出门
# en: You invent an errand, and Bram seizes it like a rope. You walk the whole length of the Crescent without a word, until your feet ache and he says, "She's the brave one. Don't tell her I said so." Then: "Don't tell him I write to her." You don't ask who. You don't need to.
opt.right.say.1 [41c747] = 你编了个差事，布拉姆像抓住救命绳一样抓住了它。你们一言不发地走完了整条新月街，走得脚都疼了，直到他说：“她才是勇敢的那个。别告诉她我这么说。”然后：“别告诉他我给她写信。”你没问是谁。你不需要问。

@event lyra_capital_promenade
# en: Being Seen
title [c9448f] = 让人看见
# en: Lyra comes down the street between two of her mother's friends, all three walking slowly to be seen, the uptown way. She looks bored in a very expensive manner. Then she sees you. {rel(lyra) >= 10: For a moment her face does something unguarded.|Her chin goes up a degree.} The ladies would like to know who you are.
text [0c97e5] = 莱拉走在街上，两旁是她母亲的两位朋友，三个人都慢悠悠地走着，好让人看见，这是上城的做派。她一副很昂贵的厌倦模样。然后她看见了你。{rel(lyra) >= 10: 那一瞬间，她脸上露出了毫无防备的神情。|她的下巴抬高了一度。}两位夫人想知道你是谁。
# en: A fashionable city street, well-dressed people strolling slowly, a proud young woman in black walking between two older ladies and glancing at a student.
scene [696e05] = 一条时髦的城市街道，衣着讲究的行人慢慢踱步，一个骄傲的黑衣年轻女子走在两位年长夫人之间，朝一个学生瞥了一眼。
# en: Bow and play along
opt.left.label [a948ad] = 鞠躬，配合她们
# en: You bow exactly as low as you should, and keep it up, stiffly, for a whole street. The ladies approve of you by a quarter-inch. Lyra introduces you as "a colleague" and gives you a look that promises an explanation later. A note comes instead, which is more.
opt.left.say.1 [35eefe] = 你的躬鞠得不多不少，恰到好处，还僵硬地保持了整整一条街。两位夫人对你的认可多了四分之一寸。莱拉把你介绍为“一位同僚”，给了你一个“回头再解释”的眼神。后来没有解释，来的是一张便条，这更难得。
# en: Ask her to walk with you
opt.right.label [f296e2] = 请她跟你一起走
# en: {rel(lyra) >= 0: The ladies stare. Lyra hesitates exactly one breath, then excuses herself "to discuss duelling". You walk three streets before she speaks. "They'll tell my mother," she says, and sounds, for once, as if she doesn't care.|"Certainly not," she says, and then does, to annoy them. The ladies talk about you all the way home. You argue about duelling for three streets, and it's the best she's looked all week.}
opt.right.say.1 [9fbd4a] = {rel(lyra) >= 0: 两位夫人瞪大了眼睛。莱拉犹豫了一口气的工夫，便借口“要讨论决斗”告退了。你们走了三条街她才开口。“她们会告诉我母亲的，”她说，难得听起来像是毫不在乎。|“当然不行，”她说，然后还是跟你走了，就为了气她们。两位夫人一路都在议论你。你们为决斗吵了三条街，这是她这一整个星期最好看的时候。}

@event mir_capital_brass
# en: Brass by the Pound
title [31f01e] = 论斤卖的黄铜
# en: Mir comes at you down the street with a sack of brass on one shoulder and a look of pure joy. "The bazaar sells scrap by the pound," they say. "By the pound! I've spent my coach fare. Probably I've spent next month. Look at this escapement, though. Look at it."
text [42d55d] = 米尔扛着一袋黄铜从街那头冲你走来，满脸纯粹的快乐。“大集市的废料论斤卖，”TA说，“论斤！我把车费花光了。大概下个月的钱也花光了。不过你看这个擒纵机构。你看看。”
# en: A busy city street, a beaming young artificer with soot-smudged goggles carrying an overflowing sack of scrap brass on one shoulder.
scene [5d811b] = 一条繁忙的城市街道，一个戴着沾满煤灰护目镜、笑容灿烂的年轻工匠，肩上扛着一个装得满满的废黄铜袋子。
# en: Pay their fare home
opt.left.label [4b2c7a] = 替TA付回家的车费
# en: You pay their coach fare home, and see the coins go with a small pang. A week later they give you a little brass beetle "for the fare", which walks in circles and, if you hum, in squares. They never explain the squares.
opt.left.say.1 [2a7497] = 你替TA付了回家的车费，看着钱币离手，心里小小一疼。一个星期后，TA送你一只小小的黄铜甲虫，“抵车费”，会绕圈走，要是你哼歌，它就走方形。TA从没解释过方形。
# en: Help carry it home
opt.right.label [6e5cb1] = 帮TA扛回家
# en: It weighs more than Mir does. You carry it across half the city to the works quarter, swapping shoulders every street, while Mir explains what each piece will become. By the Ten Clockworks you know a great deal about escapements and nothing about your left arm.
opt.right.say.1 [0ecc79] = 这袋子比米尔还重。你扛着它穿过半座城，走到工坊区，每过一条街就换一边肩膀，米尔一路给你讲每一块会变成什么。到滕氏钟表坊时，你对擒纵机构了解得很多，对自己的左胳膊一无所知。

@event bram_capital_no_sash
# en: Off Duty
title [8da002] = 休假中
# en: Bram Hollis on holiday looks like a door without a frame. You find him in the street, staring at a pigeon that is eating somebody's dropped pie in a plainly unauthorised manner. His hand keeps going to the ledger in his pocket. "There are no rules here," he says, appalled. "Nobody's in charge of any of it."
text [0481f6] = 放假的布拉姆·霍利斯看起来像一扇没有门框的门。你在街上碰见他，他正盯着一只鸽子，那鸽子在吃别人掉在地上的馅饼，显然未经许可。他的手不停地摸向口袋里的记过簿。“这里没有规矩，”他惊骇地说，“什么都没人管。”
# en: A busy city street, a burly young man without his prefect's sash looking lost, a fat pigeon eating a dropped pie at his feet, a notebook in his hand.
scene [30a3c3] = 一条繁忙的城市街道，一个没戴级长绶带、显得茫然的魁梧年轻人，脚边一只胖鸽子在吃掉在地上的馅饼，他手里拿着一个本子。
# en: Help him write up the pigeon
opt.left.label [49ea6f] = 帮他给鸽子记过
# en: You stand guard while Bram writes the pigeon a demerit "for loitering and theft of pastry" and tucks it under a stone. A small crowd gathers and stares at you both. Someone applauds. Bram goes red and then, to his own visible surprise, laughs.
opt.left.say.1 [a38e4c] = 你站岗放哨，布拉姆给鸽子记了一次过，“罪名：游荡及盗窃糕点”，把那页纸塞在一块石头底下。一小群人围过来盯着你们俩。有人鼓掌。布拉姆脸红了，然后，连他自己都明显吃了一惊，他笑了。
# en: Show him the city's rules
opt.right.label [e6349d] = 带他看看这城里的规矩
# en: You walk him through the lamplighters' rounds, the cab ranks, the order of the fish-market stalls: every street has rules nobody wrote down. By the bridges your feet ache, and Bram is calmer and taking notes. "Unofficial," he says. "But consistent. I can respect that."
opt.right.say.1 [0447d2] = 你带他看点灯人的巡回路线、马车的候客点、鱼市摊位的排列：每条街都有没人写下来的规矩。走到桥边时你的脚都疼了，布拉姆却平静多了，还在记笔记。“非正式的，”他说，“但是一以贯之。这我可以尊重。”

@event corvin_capital_quay
# en: Good Hands
title [ca583e] = 好手
# en: In the crowd at the fish market Corvin goes suddenly still. A young dockhand is drifting along the stalls, light-fingered: a purse here, a handkerchief there. "Good hands," Corvin murmurs. "Bad habits. She'll be caught before the year's out." He doesn't take his eyes off her. "I'm going to have a word. Coming?"
text [ea91bf] = 在鱼市的人群里，科尔文忽然一动不动。一个年轻的码头女工正顺着摊位溜达，手很轻：这儿一个钱袋，那儿一块手帕。“好手，”科尔文低声说，“坏习惯。不到年底她就会被抓。”他的眼睛一直没离开她。“我去跟她说两句。来吗？”
# en: A busy fish market on a stone quay at midday, a young dockhand slipping a purse from a stranger's coat, a lean young man watching her with an odd expression, a student beside him.
scene [652dcb] = 中午石砌码头上繁忙的鱼市，一个年轻的码头女工从陌生人的外套里摸走一个钱袋，一个精瘦的年轻人神情古怪地看着她，身边站着一个学生。
# en: Go with him
opt.left.label [6da35b] = 跟他一起去
# en: Corvin falls into step beside her and talks low and fast, as you've never heard him talk: no jokes, a lot of street names. She looks at him as if he were a ghost, and goes. "Somebody did that for me once," he says. "I didn't listen either." Then he buys you both fried fish.
opt.left.say.1 [e03da2] = 科尔文走到她身边，跟她并肩走着，低声飞快地说话，你从没听过他这样说话：不开玩笑，说了一大串街名。她看着他，像看见了鬼，然后走了。“以前有人也这么对我说过，”他说，“我也没听。”然后他给你们俩买了炸鱼。
# en: Watch how she works
opt.right.label [ca14de] = 看她怎么下手
# en: You stay by the stalls and study her, a thief's lesson taken for free: the bump, the apology, the hand already gone. You learn more in ten minutes than in a term. Corvin comes back alone, quiet, and a little cool with you for staying behind. He won't say more.
opt.right.say.1 [4a8420] = 你留在摊位边研究她，白捡了一堂小偷的课：一撞，一声抱歉，手已经收回去了。十分钟学到的比一个学期还多。科尔文一个人回来，很安静，对你留在后面有点冷淡。他再也不肯多说。

@event seraphine_capital_bells
# en: Before the Bells
title [a9c51b] = 钟响之前
# en: On the steps of the Cathedral of the Lamp, Seraphine is taking bets from a ring of cab drivers on exactly when the next bell will ring. She's winning. She's been winning for an hour. "I hear them early," she tells you, quite untroubled. "All the bells, all day. It seemed a waste." The drivers are starting to look at each other.
text [afbdeb] = 在明灯大教堂的台阶上，瑟拉芬正跟一圈马车夫打赌下一声钟什么时候响。她在赢。她已经赢了一个钟头了。“我会提前听见它们，”她毫不在意地告诉你，“所有的钟，一整天。不用白不用。”车夫们开始互相使眼色了。
# en: The wide steps of a cathedral at dusk, a dreamy young woman sitting among a ring of cab drivers holding out coins, the great spire above.
scene [e1a337] = 黄昏时大教堂宽阔的台阶，一个神情恍惚的年轻女子坐在一圈伸着手拿硬币的马车夫中间，高耸的尖塔在上方。
# en: Get her away before they twig
opt.left.label [03e11e] = 趁他们没回过神带她走
# en: You make her give back half and hurry her off, red-faced, while the drivers argue over the rest. "Was that wrong?" she asks three streets later, genuinely curious. "I did tell them I'd win. They didn't believe me. Nobody ever believes the true one."
opt.left.say.1 [a650ce] = 你让她退了一半的钱，红着脸把她拉走，车夫们还在为剩下的一半争吵。“那样不对吗？”过了三条街，她真心好奇地问，“我跟他们说过我会赢的。他们不信。从来没人信真话。”
# en: Bet on her yourself
opt.right.label [324067] = 你自己也押她
# en: You put a crown on the quarter-hour and win four. Seraphine squeezes your arm at every bell, a breath early. Then a driver works it out, and you both run down the cathedral steps laughing, pockets jingling, his shouting fading behind you.
opt.right.say.1 [1d128f] = 你押了一个克朗赌一刻钟，赢了四个。每一声钟响前一口气，瑟拉芬都会捏一下你的胳膊。然后有个车夫回过神来，你们俩笑着跑下大教堂的台阶，口袋叮当作响，他的叫骂声越来越远。
